<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baraqish</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yathill</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baraqish, ancient Yathill, is a walled ruined city in the al-Jawf region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, north-east of Sanaa. It was the second city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-main">Kingdom of Ma'in</a> and a station on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> route, enclosed by stone walls with dozens of towers. In 25–24 BCE the Roman expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelius-gallus">Aelius Gallus</a> took it on its way towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marib">Marib</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baraqish.jpg" alt="Baraqish" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: txago · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barakisk_flickr01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The town stands on a high hill in Wādī Farda in al-Jawf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a>, about 120 miles east of Sanaa. The wadi was a favoured caravan route because it had water. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> knew the town as Athlula or Athrula, from the Sabaean name yθl, probably pronounced Yathill. It was inhabited as early as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, each generation building its houses on the ruins of the one before.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 6th century BCE the city was under Saba, and a document of the Sabaean ruler Yitha'amar Bayyin II mentions a rebellion there. Inscriptions record that the Sabaeans rebuilt its wall in 450 BCE. The Minaean kingdom established itself in the 5th century BCE, and the city is named in a document dated to 343 BCE. Under the Minaean kingdom it was an important stop on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> route and a religious centre. Saba retook it in the 2nd century BCE.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelius-gallus">Aelius Gallus</a>, the general of Augustus, took the city but soon left because of disease and poor water; the tomb of a Roman cavalryman, P. Cornelius, has been found there. Hadhramaut took the city in 242. People lived in the town until the 1960s, after which it was abandoned. In 2015, while it was held by Houthi forces, the site was reported badly damaged by bombing by the Saudi Arabian military.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The wall, 14 metres high with 57 towers and two gates, is still largely visible. In the south of the city stand the ruins of a temple of 16 columns in the Minaean style, which archaeologists think was dedicated to the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athtar">Athtar</a>, with a necropolis of grave stelae beside it. Another temple in the centre keeps four standing columns. Italian archaeologists, in two campaigns begun in 1989 and 2003, excavated a temple with its roof intact, holding stone tables or altars with bulls' heads at each end, believed to belong to a sanctuary of a god of healing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baraqish” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the 6th or 5th century BCE it was independent, its kings ruling from Qarnawu with <b>Yathill</b> (Baraqish) as a second major city.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-main">Kingdom of Ma'in</a></li></ul></section>
